## The EU Grocery Shopper

  Private lable (shop brand / no brand; such as Aldi's Lacura, Specially Selected, Simply Nature) now stands at 40% of share possibly due to inflation - *the type of product is becoming equally as relevant as the brand name than the brand EXAMPLE: fyffes banana's V organic banana's
    Design Implications: The app should allow users to organise shopping lists by product type, brand preference and budget, making it easy to compare branded and private-label alternatives. Users should be able to specify what matters most to them, such as price, organic certification or product quality, rather than relying solely on brand names.

  Generally Grocery has been growing quite slowly, only growing by 3.4% in 2025 - more of a focus on AI.
    Design Implications: The app should prioritise features that provide clear value to users, such as reducing planning effort, simplifying list creation and suggesting relevant products. AI could support these tasks through personalised suggestions, list generation and predictive item reminders, while keeping users in control of their shopping decisions.

  Typically grociery stores are reviewed more positivly if online - 75% of grocery stores in the McKinsey recieved a higher score online against physical store.
    - This could be due to customers wanting different things from online V physical store
          - Physical Store: Convenence of location, low prices, promotions/ special offers
          - Online: Value for money, product quality & ease of use (UX)
      Design Implications: The app should reflect the different priorities of online and in-store shoppers. Features should support easy list management, clear product information, price comparisons and value-for-money decisions. Lists should also be practical to use in physical stores, with clear categories and an intuitive checklist that makes finding and ticking off items straightforward.

  Income changes shopping habbits in users 
    - Lower Income: Look for low-cost items
    - Higher Income: Look for premium products
    - The Middle: Is becoming smaller and smaller as wealth divide grows.
      Design Implications: The app should support different budgets through features such as price comparisons, budget tracking, affordable alternatives and the ability to prioritise essential items, allowing users to make informed purchasing decisions based on their financial needs.
 
Age also effects what you purchase
    - Gen X & Baby Boomers (generally over 40): Make their own food & shop for ingredients as well as sweet treats, etc.
    - Gen Z & Millenniums (generally under 40): Eat grab-and-go meals (this doesnt mean unhealthy, just that they want it fast)
      Design Implications: The app should accommodate both ingredient-led and convenience-led shopping. Users should be able to plan meals and organise ingredients, as well as quickly add prepared meals, snacks and ready-to-eat products, without assumptions about their preferences based on age.

  12% of EU customers sechduel weekly deliveries of groceries this number is currently variable, but set to increase slightly by 2030. 
  69% of EU customers sechduel deliveries *less than once a month*

  *Nice to have's in the app*
    - Set reminders for frequently purchased products -  quickly
    - Target shops that don't offer delivery / offer expensive delivery, or offer to keep a list that to keep on top of their basic essentials and long term essentials (pasta / non perishables) *target the often forgotten*
    - Emphasise the ease of multi-user use
    - Shopping list templates / reusable lists - occasion-specific lists, ability to duplicate list's / list items, highly editable lists at any stage
    - Intergrate lists with delivery apps like Uber Eats, Tesco & Picnic
